ts, split each part with more than one level into\ntwo, and continue until every part is a single level. The <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"g-1\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D454 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2212\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> contrasts of the splits form a\ncomplete set of orthogonal contrasts, whatever the group sizes.</p></li>\n</ol>\n</div>","file":"ch15-anova-subspaces/04-orthogonal-contrasts.html"}